Suppose  vector database contains 1 million document vectors.

A user asks:

How does FastAPI async work?

The embedding model converts it into:

Question Vector

[0.12, 0.44, 0.78]

Your vector database contains:

Document A

[0.13, 0.45, 0.79]
Document B

[-0.82, 0.12, -0.56]

Now the question is:

How does FAISS decide which document is most similar?

It calculates the cosine similarity between the query vector and every document vector.

The document with the highest cosine similarity is considered the most relevant.

Instead of comparing the lengths of the arrows, cosine similarity compares their directions.

If two vectors point in almost the same direction:

They have similar meaning.

If they point in opposite directions:

They have very different meanings.

Range

Cosine similarity is always between:

| Score | Meaning                  |
| ----- | ------------------------ |
| +1    | Almost identical meaning |
| 0.8   | Very similar             |
| 0.5   | Somewhat related         |
| 0     | Unrelated                |
| -1    | Opposite direction       |


Why Not Euclidean Distance?

Suppose:

Document A

[1,1]

Document B

[100,100]

The vectors are very different in length.

But they point in exactly the same direction.

Their meaning is likely very similar.

Cosine similarity captures this.

Euclidean distance focuses on absolute distance instead.

For semantic search, direction (meaning) is usually more important than magnitude.
